          "question": "Burning when I pee and going more often since yesterday, no temperature.",
          "answer": "Your symptoms, burning when you pee and needing to go more often since yesterday without a fever, were checked against the emergency guidance I can access. No emergency pattern matched. That is not the same as being cleared. The check only covers a fixed list of conditions, so please talk to a clinician soon, especially if things are not improving.\n\nThe \"Urinary tract infection\" reference (general clinical reference) describes burning, frequent urination, and urgency as the usual features of a lower urinary tract infection. It also says these symptoms alone do not confirm one, and only a clinician can assess that. The same document says fever, chills, back or side (flank) pain, nausea, vomiting, or feeling generally unwell point toward a more serious upper tract infection. Those would change how urgently you should be seen.\n\nThe \"Sepsis: signs of a life-threatening response to infection\" guidance (critical care and emergency medicine society) warns that a normal temperature is not reassuring on its own, and that older adults may have no fever at all. For older adults, the signs can be confusion, a fall, or a sharp drop in how well they function.\n\n**Seek urgent or emergency care now if you have any of these:**\n- Fever, shaking chills, or feeling very unwell\n- Pain in your back or side\n- Vomiting, or being unable to keep fluids down\n- New confusion, unusual drowsiness, or a fall\n- Blood in your urine with any of the above\n\n**Otherwise,** contact a doctor, nurse line, or pharmacist today, or within a day, to describe your symptoms. I can't tell you what treatment is right for you.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
